plane. The first order event plane then has its angle \(\Psi^{(1)}_{\rm PP}\) (participant plane angle in Fig. 1.14). Due to fluctuations of the shapes of the colliding nuclei, it is also possible to define target and projectile spectator planes (\(\Psi^{\rm t}_{\rm SP}\) and \(\Psi^{\rm p}_{\rm SP}\)). Because of such fluctuations the angles are generally non-zero and different for each individual heavy-ion collision and lead to a rapidity-even contribution to \(v_{1}\), where \(v^{\rm even}_{1}(y)=v^{\rm even}_{1}(-y)\).

Experimentally, the \(v^{\rm odd}_{1}\) is often studied as a function of the collision energy. Exam

