#### Selection criteria

The next step after the event selection described in Section 4.1 is track selection and subsequent particle identification (PID). Because of long lifetime of the D\({}^{\pm}\) mesons, all global tracks are used for the analysis. Multiple selection criteria are applied to the tracks to ensure their good quality, which are listed in Tab. 4.6. The lower cut on \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) for all tracks is used in order to reduce the combinatorial background in the low \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) region. This is very important because of the three body decay used for the D\({}^{\pm}\) reconstruction. The cut on \(\eta\) is given by the STAR detector geometry. The following two cuts on number of hits in the TPC ensure that only good quality TPC tracks are analyzed. And finally it is required, that each track has properly matched signal in the HFT which for Run16 means that there is a hit in at least three layers of the HFT: one hit in the PXL1, one hit in the PXL2 and at least one hit in the IST or SSD.

Next, the tracks are identified using standard procedures at STAR. The kaons and pions are identified in the TPC based on a cut on their energy loss \(\mathrm{d}E/\mathrm{d}x\) in the TPC gas by cutting on the n\(\sigma\). The TOF is used to help with the PID based on a cut on \(|1/\beta-1/\beta_{\mathrm{TOF}}|\), where \(\beta\) is particle velocity measured determined from its momentum and \(\beta_{\mathrm{TOF}}\) is a velocity measured by the TOF. Figure 4.3 shows the \(\mathrm{d}E/\mathrm{d}x\) (left) and \(1/\beta\) (right) distributions from the TPC and TOF for Run16 data-set.

