In case of the TPC PID efficiency, it was decided to use a constant function to fit the PID efficiency data points despite the shape of the \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) dependency in order to simplify the procedure. The reason why this should not pose serious problems is that majority of pions coming from decays of \(\mathrm{D}^{\pm}\) mesons in this analysis have \(p_{\mathrm{T}}<2\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\). In that region, the TPC PID efficiency is practically constant in \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\), so the constant fit captures the shape well. Another argument is, that PID plays an important role in the low-\(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) region. For \(p_{\mathrm{T}}>2\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\), the topological selection will play much more significant role in \(\mathrm{D}^{\pm}\) reconstruction.

The general strategy for \(\mathrm{K}^{\pm}\) meson PID efficiency is the same as for \(\pi\). The only difference is in the way the pure \(\mathrm{K}^{\pm}\) was obtained. There is a suitable decay, which can be used, specifically decay of \(\phi\to\mathrm{K}^{+}\mathrm{K}^{-}\), but its reconstruction using just decay topology and PID on one of the decay daughters was not successful in the Run16 data-set. For that reason a different strategy was implemented.

For determination of the TPC PID efficiency, the pure sample of \(\mathrm{K}^{\pm}\) mesons was selected using a strict PID cut in the TOF and vice versa for the TOF PID efficiency. Specifically, the strict TOF cut used is \(|1/\beta-1/\beta_{\mathrm{K}}|<0.01\) and the strict TPC cut is \(|\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{K}}|<0.5\). The rest of the calculation is the same as for \(\pi^{\pm}\) mesons, i.e. the \(\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{K}}\) and \(|1/\beta-1/\beta_{\mathrm{K}}|\) distributions are filled in 16 bins, this time covering \(0<p_{\mathrm{T}}<2\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\). The reasons, why it should not be a problem to use this more limited \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) range are the same as for the fit of the TPC PID efficiency of \(\pi^{\pm}\) mesons, as discussed above.

The fitted PID distributions for \(\mathrm{K}^{\pm}\) mesons are shown in Fig. 4.20 and 4.21. The \(\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{K}}\) distributions look quite similar to those of \(\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\pi}\). On the other hand, the \(|1/\beta-1/\beta_{\mathrm{K}}|\) show clearly significant level of contamination with other hadrons (pions and protons,

