### 4.4 Results

This section provides a summary of the combined results from the measurement of D\({}^{\pm}\) mesons in Run14 and Run16 Au+Au collisions \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=200\) GeV. Each of the following sub-sections describes procedures used to calculate individual observables.

#### Invariant spectrum of D\({}^{\pm}\) mesons

The invariant spectra of D\({}^{\pm}\) mesons in Run16 Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=200\) GeV are calculated from the raw yields (\(Y_{\rm raw}\)) listed in Tabs. 4.9, 4.10, and 4.11 and using reconstruction efficiency (\(\varepsilon_{\rm reco}\)) from Fig. 4.25 using the following formula

\[\frac{{\rm d}^{2}N}{2\pi p_{\rm T}{\rm d}p_{\rm T}{\rm d}y}=\frac{Y_{\rm raw}}{2 \pi p_{\rm T}\Delta p_{\rm T}\Delta y\cdot BR\cdot\varepsilon_{\rm reco}}, \tag{4.18}\]

where \(BR=(9.38\pm 0.16)\,\%\)[76] is the branching ratio, \(\Delta p_{\rm T}\) is the bin width, and \(\Delta y\) is the rapidity window. The D\({}^{\pm}\) mesons were reconstructed in \(|y|<1\) and consequently \(\Delta y=2\).

The Run16 invariant spectrum is subsequently combined with the Run14 spectrum. This is done as a weighted average for each point in \(p_{\rm T}\), where the weights are \(w_{\rm Run14}=1/\sigma_{\rm stat,Run14}^{2}\) and \(w_{\rm Run16}=1/\sigma_{\rm stat,Run16}^{2}\). The statistical uncertainty of the combined spectrum is calculated as follows

\[\sigma_{\rm stat,tot}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{w_{\rm Run14}+w_{\rm Run16}}}. \tag{4.19}\]

The combined invariant spectrum of D\({}^{\pm}\) mesons is plotted in Fig. 4.34 for three centrality classes of Au+Au collisions. The \(10-40\%\) and \(40-80\%\) spectra are scaled by a constant for plotting. The data are fitted with the Levy function:

\[\frac{{\rm d}^{2}N}{2\pi p_{\rm T}{\rm d}p_{\rm T}{\rm d}y}=\frac{1}{2\pi} \frac{{\rm d}N}{{\rm d}y}\frac{(A-1)(A-2)}{AB(AB+m_{0}(A-2))}\left[1+\frac{ \sqrt{p_{\rm T}^{2}+m_{0}^{2}}-m_{0}}{AB}\right]^{-A}, \tag{4.20}\]

where \({\rm d}N/{\rm d}y\), \(A\) and \(B\) are free parameters of the fit and \(m_{0}\) is the rest mass of the D\({}^{\pm}\) meson. The fits are plotted as dashed lines.

#### Nuclear modification factor

The combined invariant spectrum of \(\rm D^{\pm}\) mesons described in the previous section is used to calculate the nuclear modification factor \(R_{\rm AA}\), defined using Eq. (1.4). No measurement of \(\rm D^{\pm}\) mesons in p+p collisions by STAR is currently available, so the measurement of \(\rm c\bar{c}\) cross section in p+p collisions at \(\sqrt{s}=200\,\)GeV [163] was used instead. The \(\rm c\bar{c}\) is first scaled to the invariant yield of \(\rm D^{0}\) which is achieved by using a scaling factor \(f(c\to D^{0})/\sigma_{\rm pp}^{\rm inel}\), where \(f(c\to D^{0})=0.6086\) is the \(\rm D^{0}\) fragmentation function and \(\sigma_{\rm pp}^{\rm inel}\) is the total inelastic p+p cross section.

Next, this invariant spectrum of \(\rm D^{0}\) in p+p is scaled by the \(\rm D^{\pm}/\rm D^{0}\) yield ratio from PYTHIA. As shown in Fig. 4.35, three different PYTHIA versions were chosen based on discussion within the STAR Heavy Flavor Physics Working Group. In general, all three versions use a tune for heavy flavor production at RHIC energies. PYTHA 6.4 Perugia 2012 and PYTHIA 8.1 were both thoroughly tuned on STAR p+p data in order to ensure good description of various observables measured in p+p collisions at STAR (not only heavy favor production, but also jets). The tune for PYTHA 8.2 was a bit simpler, focusing primarily on description of open-charm production in p+p collisions. An average of the three PYTHIA versions from Fig. 4.35 was used for the scaling of the p+p reference. Like this, the \(\rm D^{\pm}\) invariant spectrum in p+p collisions is estimated and is used for the \(R_{\rm AA}\) calculation. The reason to use this procedure is that in the first step, the \(\rm c\bar{c}\) is scaled to \(\rm D^{0}\) invariant spectrum, which was used as a reference in the published \(\rm D^{0}\)\(R_{\rm AA}\) measurement [2]. In the next step the \(\rm D^{0}\) invariant spectrum in p+p

Figure 4.34: Invariant spectra of \(\rm D^{\pm}\) mesons measured in 0-10%, 10-40%, and 40-80% central Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=200\) GeV. The data are fitted with tge Levy function.

collisions is scaled to \(\mathrm{D}^{\pm}\) invariant spectrum using \(\mathrm{D}^{\pm}/\mathrm{D}^{0}\) yield ratio from PYTHIA, taking into account difference in shape of \(\mathrm{D}^{\pm}\) and \(\mathrm{D}^{0}\)\(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) spectra. Finally, the mean number of binary collisions \(\langle N_{\mathrm{coll}}\rangle\) is taken from Tab. 4.2.

The \(R_{\mathrm{AA}}\) of \(\mathrm{D}^{\pm}\) mesons is plotted in Fig. 4.36. The data are compared to the \(\mathrm{D}^{0}\) measurement in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\mathrm{NN}}}=200\,\mathrm{GeV}\) from STAR [2] and to \(\mathrm{D}^{\pm}\) mesons measured by ALICE in \(0-10\%\) central Pb+Pb collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\mathrm{NN}}}=5.02\,\mathrm{TeV}\)[117]. All data-sets are in a good agreement and they all show a significant suppression of open-charm mesons in central and mid-central heavy-ion collisions in the high-\(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) region.

As expected, the \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) and centrality dependence of the \(\mathrm{D}^{\pm}\) mesons is essentially the same as for previously measured \(\mathrm{D}^{0}\) mesons. The suppression at high transverse momenta is the largest in central Au+Au collisions and gets weaker when going to mid-central and peripheral collisions. In \(40-80\%\) centrality class, there is no suppression observed at high \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) within the uncertainties.

The low \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) D-mesons exhibit a significant suppression as well, but in this case independent of collision centrality. As shown in several STAR open-charm studies [3, 4], this observation can be likely contributed to redistribution of c-quarks among open-charm hadron species due to coalescence hadronization of c-quarks in the QGP.

