## 2 Open-Charm Measurements with the HFT

The main sub-systems for reconstruction of open heavy-flavor hadrons in STAR are the Time Projection Chamber (TPC) which is used for momentum determination and for particle identification, the Time Of Flight (TOF) which improves the particle identification, and the HFT which enables precise reconstruction of the decay topology.

To reconstruct the open-charm hadrons, a series of selection criteria has to be applied to the events and tracks first. The specific selection of the topological variables and values of the criteria depend on the open-charm hadron species and its decay channel. After applying all the selection criteria, the open-charm hadron raw yields (\(Y_{\text{raw}}\)) are extracted from the invariant mass spectrum. The invariant yield is then calculated from \(Y_{\text{raw}}\) as:

\[\frac{\text{d}^{2}N}{2\pi p_{\text{T}}\text{d}p_{\text{T}}\text{d}y}=\frac{Y_{ \text{raw}}}{2\pi N_{\text{evt}}BR_{\text{P}}\text{d}p_{\text{T}}\Delta y \varepsilon(p_{\text{T}})}, \tag{1}\]

where \(N_{\text{evt}}\) is number of events, \(BR\) is the branching ratio, \(p_{\text{T}}\) is the transverse momentum, \(y\) is the rapidity and \(\varepsilon(p_{\text{T}})\) is the reconstruction efficiency. The nuclear modification factor (\(R_{\text{AA}}\)) is subsequently calculated according to formula:

\[R_{\text{AA}}(p_{\text{T}})=\frac{\text{d}N^{\text{AA}}/\text{d}p_{\text{T}}} {\langle N_{\text{coll}}\rangle\text{d}N^{\text{PP}}/\text{d}p_{\text{T}}}, \tag{2}\]

where \(\text{d}N^{\text{AA}}/\text{d}p_{\text{T}}\) and \(\text{d}N^{\text{PP}}/\text{d}p_{\text{T}}\) are the invariant yields measured in heavy-ion collisions and p+p collisions respectively and \(\langle N_{\text{coll}}\rangle\) is the mean number of binary nucleon-nucleon collisions computed from the Glauber model. The results presented in this proceedings use a combined measurement of \(\text{D}^{\star}\) and \(\text{D}^{0}\) in p+p collisions at \(\sqrt{s}=200\,\text{GeV}\) measured by the STAR experiment in 2009 [4] as a reference.

Figure 1 shows the nuclear modification factor \(R_{\text{AA}}\) of \(\text{D}^{0}\) and \(\text{D}^{\pm}\) mesons as a function of transverse momentum \(p_{\text{T}}\) for 0-10% central Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\text{NN}}}=200\,\text{GeV}\). As expected, the level of suppression of \(\text{D}^{0}\) and \(\text{D}^{\pm}\) is similar.

